---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { layers, localUrl } from "../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture — OrbitKV"
  description="Engine-owned HBM, per-host Managers, inventory-fed indexes, leased etcd membership and OrbiTE cache transfers."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <div class="container interior">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Architecture / Ownership before placement</p>
      <h1>
        One cache API.
        <br />
        <span class="highlight">Across tiers.</span>
      </h1>
      <p>
        The engine owns GPU memory and model execution. OrbitKV owns external
        cache replicas, finds reusable prefixes, and holds pages until transfers
        finish.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="detail-section" aria-labelledby="topology-title">
      <h2 id="topology-title">From one host to a shared cache</h2>
      <figure class="architecture-figure">
        <img
          src={localUrl("/architecture.svg")}
          width="1280"
          height="850"
          alt="Engine-owned HBM and raw DRAM Restore; Manager-owned Publish, SSD and codecs. Peer grants control OrbiTE READs; inventory streams feed local indexes and etcd holds membership. Native engine backends own P/D."
        />
        <figcaption>
          Implemented ownership paths, not a qualification claim. OrbiTE owns
          local, peer and file byte movement; each Manager owns its cache and
          explicitly covered inventory views. A shared API does not make KV
          layouts interchangeable. <a href={localUrl("/docs/compatibility/")}>All current GPU/RDMA/GDS and installed profiles are unqualified.</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="layer-grid">
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">01 / Single node</span>
          <h3>Keep useful prefixes.</h3>
          <p>
            Run an independent Manager per node and connect engines to its
            shared DRAM and optional SSD cache. Engines retain HBM ownership
            and execute raw DRAM restores through imported payload arenas.
            Manager workers retain Publish, SSD and encoded restores.
            Standalone deployment needs neither etcd nor peer gRPC. Configure
            the SSD path and capacity. OrbiTE consumes file layout vectors and
            owns io_uring staging or explicitly configured GDS. OrbitKV retains
            the index, extents and leases; no native SSD I/O fallback remains.
            See <a href={localUrl("/docs/gds/")}>file transport limits</a>.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">02 / Independent replicas</span>
          <h3>Reuse across workers.</h3>
          <p>
            Managers discover compatible replicas in complete local global indexes,
            synchronized through bounded inventory streams. etcd maintains leased
            membership; peer gRPC authorizes exact source replicas. OrbiTE READ
            targets GPU memory for restore and DRAM for cache fills. The engine
            owns staging. <a href={localUrl("/docs/fault-qualification/#shared-cache-gates")}>Peer recovery gates</a>{" "}
            do not qualify OrbiTE; current hardware and HA gates remain open.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="layer">
          <span class="package-name">03 / Prefill &amp; decode</span>
          <h3>Compose the handoff.</h3>
          <p>
            Official vLLM NIXL/MultiConnector and SGLang native disaggregation own
            current-request handoff and transport. OrbitKV supplies independent
            cache adapters. Cache reuse has a separate
            source grant and completion contract.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>How a request reuses KV</h2>
      <ol class="compile-flow">
        <li>
          <h3>Compile</h3>
          <p>
            The adapter declares prefix, window and checkpoint rules. The
            shared contract turns them into page requirements for known token
            ranges from a valid HBM origin.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Prepare</h3>
          <p>
            The manager finds DRAM, SSD or remote candidates within query byte
            budgets. The same compiled requirements validate leased evidence
            and yield complete recovery boundaries across ranks.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Restore</h3>
          <p>
            Compiled ranges validate the state declared by a supported adapter.
            External hybrid model profiles need separate lifecycle qualification.
            Leases hold pages through transfer completion.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Continue</h3>
          <p>
            The engine resumes computation; completed KV can be published for
            later reuse.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        The vLLM adapter defers new admissions until an admitted restore reaches
        computation, avoiding deferred-queue capacity stalls. SGLang waits for
        leased readiness before allocating restore destinations. Each retains
        its own scheduler and HBM allocator.
      </p>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        Shared page requirements remove duplicate adapter arithmetic. They do
        not predict future tokens, analyze arbitrary model graphs or authorize
        reclamation. Automatic hybrid warming remains disabled. Synthetic GPU-byte
        controls alone do not qualify complete released hybrid recovery or its
        latency benefit. <a href={localUrl("/docs/performance/")}>Historical TENT performance</a>{" "}
        remains tied to its original revisions. Current OrbiTE hardware paths
        require fresh qualification.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href={localUrl("/docs/architecture/")}>
        Read the architecture and ownership contracts →
      </a>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Implementation boundaries</h2>
      <div class="definition-list">
        {layers.map((layer) => (
          <div>
            <span class="package-name">{layer.name}</span>
            <p>{layer.detail}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="detail-section">
      <h2>Contracts and future work</h2>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        Rust records bounded, opt-in route costs. Experimental selectors cover
        matching peer owners and equal-coverage host routes. The completion
        plan records implementation status and qualification; a design or CPU
        control does not authorize a broader serving profile.
      </p>
      <p class="reading-copy">
        Native P/D fault behavior, independent failure domains and current
        OrbiTE hardware profiles remain unqualified. Preparation stays opt-in.
        The delivery plan prioritizes official engine integration, persistent SSD,
        cluster discovery and upstream acceptance.
      </p>
      <div class="doc-links">
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/completion-plan/")}>
          Implementation plan and upstream references <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/architecture/#planning-and-cost-observations")}>
          Transfer costs and deployment policies <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/distributed-cache/")}>
          Distributed index design <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/state-identity/")}>
          Model-aware identity <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/completion-plan/#standard-and-complete-checklist")}>
          Standard cache capability checklist <span>→</span>
        </a>
        <a href={localUrl("/docs/completion-plan/")}>
          Completion and qualification gates <span>→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
